WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:02.629 --> 00:00:08.540
Try to act surprised: Al Pacino didn't really
snort cocaine in Scarface. Just as shootouts

00:00:08.540 --> 00:00:13.570
in movies are all staged, the countless drugs
consumed on-screen over the years aren't real.

00:00:13.570 --> 00:00:17.920
Prop masters have come up with all kinds of
crazy concoctions to make cinematic contraband

00:00:17.920 --> 00:00:22.060
look as close as possible to the real thing.
We're about to narc on all the fake drugs

00:00:22.060 --> 00:00:23.909
you see on the silver screen.

00:00:23.909 --> 00:00:29.579
Oh, and just so we're clear? Don't take any
drugs—authentic or otherwise.

00:00:29.579 --> 00:00:32.750
"Don't smoke crack"

00:00:32.750 --> 00:00:33.640
Marijuana

00:00:33.640 --> 00:00:38.120
Let's start things off with weed. Veteran
Hollywood prop master Jeff Butcher revealed

00:00:38.120 --> 00:00:43.660
to Upvoted that he's ordered fake marijuana—also
known hilariously as "wizard weed"—from

00:00:43.660 --> 00:00:48.470
a company called International Oddities so
he could stuff bags of movie weed. How kind

00:00:48.470 --> 00:00:53.250
is their fake bud? They provided the prop
marijuana in Pineapple Express on James Franco's

00:00:53.250 --> 00:00:55.040
personal recommendation.

00:00:55.040 --> 00:01:00.210
For scenes featuring joints, most movies use
either wizard weed or herbal cigarettes. While

00:01:00.210 --> 00:01:03.650
we're on the subject, remember that awesome
scene in Zombieland, where the characters

00:01:03.650 --> 00:01:05.329
get high and do...well...this?

00:01:05.329 --> 00:01:08.449
"See you on the other side Pete"

00:01:08.449 --> 00:01:11.520
"It's so disgusting!"

00:01:11.530 --> 00:01:14.009
"Don't cross the streams!"

00:01:14.009 --> 00:01:18.439
Sorry to disappoint you, but that smoke was
produced by the insides of plain ol' herbal

00:01:18.439 --> 00:01:22.969
cigarettes, dumped into a hookah. The fake
stuff might not leave you craving Funyuns,

00:01:22.969 --> 00:01:25.979
but it certainly looks the part.

00:01:25.979 --> 00:01:26.409
Cocaine

00:01:26.409 --> 00:01:31.549
The Hollywood version of coke is pretty harmless—to
an extent. During a Q&amp;A session hosted by

00:01:31.549 --> 00:01:35.439
Bryan Cranston, Jonah Hill revealed just what
happened to him on the set of The Wolf of

00:01:35.439 --> 00:01:38.209
Wall Street after he snorted too much of the
fake stuff:

00:01:38.209 --> 00:01:44.030
"I got bronchitis for a month and a half horribly,
because the cocaine that we snorted was vitamin

00:01:44.030 --> 00:01:49.920
B, and I had snorted so much vitamin B that
my lungs had gotten infected with bronchitis

00:01:49.920 --> 00:01:52.100
and I was sick for a month and a half."

00:01:52.100 --> 00:01:56.240
And while Jeff Butcher says that vitamin B
is one of the most commonly used cocaine substitutes

00:01:56.240 --> 00:02:00.439
for movies, prop masters have turned to all
kinds of other alternatives when making on-set

00:02:00.439 --> 00:02:04.849
powder. Other films have used mixtures of
baking soda, powdered sugar, and powdered

00:02:04.849 --> 00:02:09.590
milk to get things just right. But Scarface
co-star Steven Bauer once revealed just what

00:02:09.590 --> 00:02:13.030
it was that covered Tony Montana's desk: baby
laxative.

00:02:13.030 --> 00:02:17.530
That explains a lot. If you had to snort that
much baby laxative, you'd probably react this

00:02:17.530 --> 00:02:17.890
way too:

00:02:17.890 --> 00:02:23.460
"Say hello to my little friend!"

00:02:23.460 --> 00:02:23.950
Heroin

00:02:23.950 --> 00:02:27.900
Just like Hollywood's fake cocaine, common
household items and cooking ingredients are

00:02:27.900 --> 00:02:32.720
often mixed and put into baggies to simulate
heroin. Of course, things get a little trickier

00:02:32.720 --> 00:02:36.080
when movies show people taking close-up injections
of the junk.

00:02:36.080 --> 00:02:40.130
In an interview with The Daily Beast, propmaster
Gordon Fitzgerald explained how they filmed

00:02:40.130 --> 00:02:45.200
the heroin injection scenes in Danny Boyle's
Trainspotting from 1996. Fitzgerald recalled

00:02:45.200 --> 00:02:49.710
taking a slightly-dyed liquid and injecting
it into an artificial arm. Of course, these

00:02:49.710 --> 00:02:53.570
prosthetics have make-up and small amounts
of hair applied—and even fake blood—to

00:02:53.570 --> 00:02:57.170
make you forget you're watching make-believe.

00:02:57.170 --> 00:02:57.360
Cigarettes

00:02:57.360 --> 00:03:01.870
For decades, cigarettes featured on film were
the real thing—until the government and

00:03:01.870 --> 00:03:06.090
tobacco companies clashed over product placement
rules for potentially harmful substances.

00:03:06.090 --> 00:03:10.150
Studies found that, for younger viewers, watching
characters smoke in movies often increased

00:03:10.150 --> 00:03:12.910
the likelihood of picking up the habit in
real life.

00:03:12.910 --> 00:03:16.850
Faking cigarette smoking is a lot tougher
than you might realize. Just as they're used

00:03:16.850 --> 00:03:21.290
to simulate pot, herbal cigarettes have become
the go-to choice, especially for shows like

00:03:21.290 --> 00:03:25.820
Mad Men. But sometimes other alternatives
are necessary. Jeff Butcher explained that

00:03:25.820 --> 00:03:30.120
the prop department on 2014's Non-Stop made
hand-rolled cigarettes filled with chamomile

00:03:30.120 --> 00:03:33.720
tea for former-smoker Liam Neeson.

00:03:33.720 --> 00:03:34.100
Alcohol

00:03:34.100 --> 00:03:38.380
While alcohol is perfectly legal, you don't
want your actors getting sloshed after the

00:03:38.380 --> 00:03:42.960
twelfth take. Various teas are served at room
temperature to simulate the darker forms of

00:03:42.960 --> 00:03:44.090
the hard stuff.

00:03:44.090 --> 00:03:48.540
To replace beer, films usually just rely on
non-alcoholic brews, like O'Douls.

00:03:48.540 --> 00:03:52.780
Clear drinks like vodka tend to be just water,
but off-color shots usually have to be a mix

00:03:52.780 --> 00:03:55.590
of different liquids, like apple juice and
Pellegrino.

00:03:55.590 --> 00:04:00.940
Then again, rumor has it that John Belushi's
whiskey chug in Animal House was real, and

00:04:00.940 --> 00:04:03.790
we can't help loving him even more for it.

00:04:03.790 --> 00:04:07.200
"Ah, thanks, I needed that."

00:04:07.200 --> 00:04:07.870
Meth

00:04:07.870 --> 00:04:12.700
The Hollywood version of crank, ice, meth—or
whatever the kids call it nowadays—isn't

00:04:12.700 --> 00:04:17.100
real either. A behind-the-scenes AMC video
reveals that the meth featured on Breaking

00:04:17.100 --> 00:04:20.500
Bad is actually... homemade rock candy.

00:04:20.500 --> 00:04:24.380
And as actor Bryan Cranston explains in this
Entertainment Weekly video, the sweet stuff

00:04:24.380 --> 00:04:26.130
can be just as addictive:

00:04:26.130 --> 00:04:35.240
"But on nights when it's labor intensive and
16th hour we start taking a little sugar."

00:04:35.240 --> 00:04:39.370
Of course, getting people hooked on rock candy
makes perfect sense for the guy who used to

00:04:39.370 --> 00:04:40.750
play a TV dentist.

00:04:40.750 --> 00:04:44.360
Thanks for watching! Subscribe to our YouTube
channel to watch more videos like the one

00:04:44.360 --> 00:04:47.970
you just saw. And leave us a comment to let
us know which item on this list fooled you

00:04:47.970 --> 00:04:48.470
the most...

